  "note": "Covers both the ClickUp web app and the Windows/Mac desktop app; the shortcuts are the same keymap in both per the vendor's articles. The main article's 'Docs shortcuts' section is only a link-out with no inline table, so Docs actions (context \"Docs\") were pulled from the dedicated Docs shortcuts article instead. The main article's 'Whiteboard shortcuts' section is likewise only a link-out, and its target (help.clickup.com/hc/en-us/articles/6326559547031-Use-Whiteboards-keyboard-shortcuts) now redirects to the 'Intro to Whiteboards' overview page, which has no shortcut table -- skipped, nothing to extract. Not expressible in the chord grammar, so left out: 'Mention (tag) a user' (@), 'Mention a task' (@@), and 'Mention a Doc' (@@@) use '@', which is not a supported punctuation key; 'Add an emoji (while in text editor)' uses ':', also unsupported. 'Create a heading' in the Text editor section is documented only as a range (\"Option+Cmd+1 to 4\" / \"Alt+Ctrl+1 to 4\"), not a single chord, so it is skipped there in favor of the four individual Heading 1-4 actions under the Docs context, taken from the dedicated Docs article -- note that article gives Windows Ctrl+Shift+1-4 rather than the Alt+Ctrl+1-4 implied by the general Text editor section; the two ClickUp pages disagree and the Docs-specific article (which lists each level individually) was used. 'Create and open subtask' is skipped: the vendor's own table swaps the platform labels (\"Cmd + Enter on PC\" / \"Ctrl + Return on Mac\"), which is self-contradictory, so left out rather than guessed. Rows documenting two alternate bindings for one action (Open Search/Command Center: S, and Cmd+K/Ctrl+K; Open Notepad: N and P; Open or close notification: O, and Return/Enter; Next notification: Down arrow and J; Previous notification: Up arrow and K) are each split into a primary action and an '(alternate)' action, since one action holds only one chord. 'Navigate between tasks in a List' documents one row for 'Control+Shift+Left arrow or Right arrow'; split into separate previous/next actions. 'Assign task to self' and 'Add task to Priorities' are each documented twice with different bindings depending on context (Task vs. Views) and are listed once per context.",
